<p align="center">
This project is designed to build, run, and manage <strong>Sonatype Nexus Repository Manager</strong> on Ubuntu using Docker.
</p>

## 📌 About the Project
This repository contains the Dockerfile, scripts, and documentation required to deploy Nexus Repository Manager.
The goal of the project is to provide a quick and reproducible way to set up Nexus on Ubuntu.

## ✨ Features
Build a Nexus image with Docker

Run the service on Ubuntu

Manage ports and volumes

Document the installation and troubleshooting steps

Ready for use in DevOps environments

Build the Docker image:

```bash
docker build -t nexus-app .
```

Run the container:
```bash
docker run -d \
  --name nexus \
  -p 8081:8081 \
  nexus-app
```
Then, open the following URL in your browser:

```text
http://localhost:8081
```
## 📁 Project Structure

```text
project/
├── Dockerfile
├── nexus-3.98.1-01-linux-x86_64.tar.gz
└── README.md
```

## 📚Things I Learned
1.
Your Dockerfile should do the following:

1-Choose a Linux base image.

2-Install Java.

3-Download and extract Nexus.

4-Create a user and data directory.

5-Expose port 8081.

6-Run Nexus with the nexus run command.

2.
In both the shell and a Dockerfile, spaces separate arguments:

```bash
mkdir -p /opt/nexus /nexus-data
```
Therefore, this command creates two separate directories:

```text
/opt/nexus
/nexus-data
```
mkdir can create multiple directories at the same time:

```bash
mkdir -p dir1 dir2 dir3
```
So it creates three directories.
3.
The options in this command:

```bash
groupadd -r nexus
useradd -r -g nexus -s /bin/false nexus
```
mean the following:

-r: creates a system account for a service, not for a human user. For Nexus, this is the correct and more secure choice.

-s /bin/false: disables the shell for this user.

-d /opt/nexus: sets /opt/nexus as the user’s home directory for the service.

-g nexus: sets nexus as the user’s primary group.

4.
Find the Package That Provides a Command Using apt-file search
apt install apt-file
apt-file update
apt-file search bin/tail


## ⚠️ Errors and Solutions
1.
I encountered the following error:
cannot create directory '../sonatype-work': Permission denied
This error means that the nexus user does not have permission to create the sonatype-work directory, or the directory already exists but is not owned by the nexus user.

The key lines are:
```
mkdir -p /opt/nexus /opt/sonatype-work
chown -R nexus:nexus /opt/nexus /opt/sonatype-work
```
The official Dockerfile performs the same steps: it creates the Sonatype work directory, assigns ownership to the nexus user, moves nexus3 to /nexus-data, and creates a symbolic link.

The following line is also important:

``` ENV SONATYPE_WORK=/opt/sonatype-work
```
Nexus uses this environment variable to determine the work-directory path. If it is not set, Nexus falls back to the default relative path ../sonatype-work, which may again cause a permission error.
2.
 Download the Nexus archive manually and place it next to the Dockerfile. The archive is copied to /tmp, extracted to /opt, and then removed from /tmp after extraction.

